  },
  {
    channel: 'Paid ads (Google, Meta)',
    basics: 'A short test period to learn campaign structure, targeting, and the ad platform interface.',
    jobReady:
      'A few months of running real (even small) budgets across several campaigns before you can read data and adjust targeting without guessing.',
    hardestPart: 'Practicing with play money teaches the interface, not judgment — real budget pressure changes the decisions you make.',
  },
  {
    channel: 'Social media marketing',
    basics: 'Days to a couple of weeks to learn platform basics, content calendars, and posting cadence.',
    jobReady:
      'A few months of managing a real account with a real audience before you can defend a content strategy with actual engagement or conversion data.',
    hardestPart: 'Everyone thinks they already know this from personal use, which is exactly why the entry-level pool is the most crowded.',
  },
  {
    channel: 'Content marketing / copywriting',
    basics: 'A short stretch to learn structure, SEO-aware writing, and basic brief-following.',
    jobReady:
      'Several months to a year of writing for real briefs and real audiences before your writing reliably drives a measurable outcome, not just reads well.',
    hardestPart: 'Writing that sounds good and writing that converts are different skills, and only real audience feedback teaches the second one.',
  },
  {
    channel: 'Email marketing and CRM',
    basics: 'A focused couple of weeks to learn one platform (Mailchimp, HubSpot, or similar) and basic automation.',
    jobReady:
      'One to two months of building real sequences and reading open/click data before you can call yourself competent — the fastest channel to reach genuine competence.',
    hardestPart: 'The tool is easy; writing sequences that do not get ignored or unsubscribed from is the actual skill.',
  },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Biology',
    body: 'SEO and analytics-heavy channels reward people who can wait months for a result without losing motivation. Paid ads and social reward people who can tolerate daily volatility and adjust fast. If watching a number move slowly over months would frustrate you more than a number swinging wildly every day, that points you toward a faster-feedback channel first.',
    note: 'Pick the channel whose feedback rhythm matches your patience, not the one with the most impressive job title.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Context',
    body: 'How much runway do you actually have before you need income? Email/CRM and paid ads can produce a defensible portfolio project in a shorter stretch of time than SEO, where results are structurally slow no matter how skilled you are.',
    note: 'If you need to be interview-ready sooner rather than later, do not pick the channel with the longest results lag as your only proof.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Market',
    body: 'Check ten live job postings for the exact channel and role you want, not "digital marketing" as a category. A "Performance Marketing Executive" posting and an "SEO Analyst" posting ask for genuinely different skills and different timelines to look credible for.',
    note: 'A general "digital marketing" course prepares you for no specific posting particularly well.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Survival',
    body: 'AI tools now draft ad copy, generate first-pass content, and summarise campaign data in minutes. The channel-specific survival question is the same everywhere: can you judge whether the AI output is actually right for this audience and this budget, or are you just approving whatever it produced?',
    note: 'The safer position in every channel is owning the judgment call, not the task the AI can already do.',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Treating "digital marketing" as one 3-month finish line',
    body: 'A single course completion date does not mean you are equally ready in SEO, paid ads, social, content, and email at once. Most people are genuinely job-ready in one or two channels first, not all five simultaneously, and pretending otherwise shows up fast in an interview.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Confusing course duration with job-readiness',
    body: 'A 3 to 4 month course teaches the vocabulary and the tools. Real competence — the kind that survives a live task in an interview — typically needs several more months of hands-on work after the course ends, not instead of it.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Practicing only with fake or play-money budgets',
    body: 'A simulated ₹0 ad campaign teaches you the platform interface, not the judgment that comes from watching a real budget underperform and having to explain why. Even a small real budget, even for a friend\'s small business, teaches faster than any amount of tutorial practice.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Chasing every channel instead of proving one',
    body: 'A resume that lists SEO, paid ads, social, content, and email with no real project behind any of them reads weaker than one channel with a documented result you can explain for ten minutes. Depth in one channel gets you the first job faster than breadth across all five.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Skipping the "explain it to someone" step',
    body: 'Running a campaign and being able to explain, in under two minutes, what happened and why it matters are different skills. Agencies now specifically test for the second one in live interview tasks, not just the campaign result itself.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'How long does it take to learn digital marketing in India, honestly?',
    a: 'It depends entirely on which channel you mean and what "learn" means to you. Basic fundamentals across all channels can take a focused stretch of a few months with consistent daily practice. Genuine job-readiness — the kind that survives a live task in an agency interview — usually needs six months to a year of hands-on work with real campaigns, not just course completion. SEO and content take the longest to prove because results are structurally slow; email/CRM and paid ads can show a defensible result the fastest.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I really learn digital marketing in 3 months, like the course ads claim?',
    a: 'You can learn the vocabulary, the tools, and the basic mechanics of most channels in roughly 3 months of consistent practice. What a 3-month course rarely delivers on its own is the real-campaign judgment that gets you hired — that comes from applying the skill to a live budget, a live audience, or a live client brief, which most structured 3-month courses do not include enough of by default.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which digital marketing skill is fastest to become job-ready in?',
    a: 'Email marketing and CRM automation tend to have the shortest path to genuine competence, often one to two months of focused practice on one platform, because the tool is simple and you can build and send real sequences without needing anyone else\'s budget or audience. Paid ads and social media follow, since a small real budget or a real personal/small-business account gets you usable proof reasonably quickly. SEO and long-form content marketing take the longest because the market itself, not your skill level, controls how fast results show up.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to learn every digital marketing channel before I can get a job?',
    a: 'No. Most entry-level and mid-level digital marketing roles in India hire for depth in one or two channels, not equal fluency across five. A candidate with one real, documented SEO or paid-ads project consistently beats a candidate with surface-level exposure to every channel and proof of none. Pick one channel that fits your working style, prove it, then add a second channel once you have your first result.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What actually makes someone "job-ready" in digital marketing, beyond finishing a course?',
    a: 'Agencies and companies in India now commonly test candidates with a live task or a paid trial project instead of relying on certificates alone. Being job-ready means you can open the actual tool (Google Ads Manager, GA4, a CRM) without needing a tutorial, explain a real result in plain language, and defend the decisions you made, not just describe what a course covered.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a self-study path slower than a structured course for learning digital marketing?',
    a: 'Not necessarily slower, but it puts the structure and accountability burden on you. A structured course compresses the vocabulary and tool-familiarity stage, which self-study learners often stretch out through trial and error. But course completion alone does not close the theory-to-proof gap either way — both paths still need real campaign practice afterward to reach genuine job-readiness.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How can I speed up my digital marketing learning timeline without cutting corners?',
    a: 'Run a real (even tiny) campaign as early as possible instead of waiting until after a course finishes. Offer to manage a small business\'s Instagram page, run a ₹500 ad test, or set up one real email sequence for a friend\'s side project. Real, even small, stakes teach judgment far faster than any amount of additional theory or tutorial video.',
  },
